Below are the columns that matter for solving the task and the typical way they will be used.  

player.player_id          : join‑key to every other table that stores a player reference  
player.player_name        : final reporting ( who the top batters/bowlers are )  
match.match_id            : join‑key between match, batsman_scored, wicket_taken  
match.season_id           : season bucket in which to aggregate runs / wickets  
batsman_scored.runs_scored: numeric value to SUM for every player within a season  
batsman_scored.innings_no : optional filter (not needed here, but available)  
wicket_taken.player_out   : player dismissed – used for counting wickets  
wicket_taken.kind_out     : filter out  'run out', 'hit wicket', 'retired hurt'  
player_match.team_id      : (not required for the final result but handy if we ever need the bowler’s team)  
ball_by_ball.bowler       : could be an alternative way to derive wickets, but wicket_taken already has it aggregated  

Typical conditions that will be used  
• match.match_id = batsman_scored.match_id (or wicket_taken.match_id)  
• match.season_id = ?                                      (aggregate per season)  
• wicket_taken.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','hit wicket','retired hurt')  
• ORDER BY total_runs DESC, player_id ASC                  (tie‑breaker)  
• ORDER BY total_wkts DESC, player_id ASC                  (tie‑breaker)  

Below are ten increasingly sophisticated exploratory queries (all limited to 20 rows so they’re safe on large tables).  Each query is wrapped in SQL‑commented annotation.

```sql
-- 1. Inspect the existing seasons that appear in the data
SELECT DISTINCT season_id
FROM match
ORDER BY season_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at how runs are stored for a single random match
SELECT bs.*
FROM batsman_scored AS bs
WHERE bs.match_id = (SELECT match_id FROM match LIMIT 1)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Look at wickets with their dismissal kinds to confirm which values have to be excluded
SELECT DISTINCT kind_out
FROM wicket_taken
ORDER BY kind_out
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Aggregate total runs by player for one chosen season (say season_id = 1)
SELECT b.player_id,
       SUM(b.runs_scored) AS total_runs
FROM   batsman_scored AS b
JOIN   match           AS m ON m.match_id = b.match_id
WHERE  m.season_id = 1
GROUP  BY b.player_id
ORDER  BY total_runs DESC, b.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Aggregate total wickets by bowler for the same season (season_id = 1)
SELECT w.player_out AS bowler_id,
       COUNT(*) AS total_wkts
FROM   wicket_taken AS w
JOIN   match        AS m ON m.match_id = w.match_id
WHERE  m.season_id = 1
  AND  w.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'hit wicket', 'retired hurt')
GROUP  BY w.player_out
ORDER  BY total_wkts DESC, w.player_out
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Apply a window‑function to pick top‑3 run‑scorers for every season
SELECT season_id,
       player_id,
       total_runs
FROM (
      SELECT m.season_id,
             b.player_id,
             SUM(b.runs_scored)                       AS total_runs,
             ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY m.season_id
                                ORDER BY SUM(b.runs_scored) DESC, b.player_id) AS rk
      FROM   batsman_scored AS b
      JOIN   match          AS m ON m.match_id = b.match_id
      GROUP  BY m.season_id, b.player_id
     )
WHERE rk <= 3
ORDER BY season_id, rk
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Same idea: window‑function to pick top‑3 wicket‑takers (dismissal filter included)
SELECT season_id,
       bowler_id,
       total_wkts
FROM (
      SELECT m.season_id,
             w.player_out                             AS bowler_id,
             COUNT(*)                                AS total_wkts,
             ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY m.season_id
                                ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, w.player_out) AS rk
      FROM   wicket_taken AS w
      JOIN   match        AS m ON m.match_id = w.match_id
      WHERE  w.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'hit wicket', 'retired hurt')
      GROUP  BY m.season_id, w.player_out
     )
WHERE rk <= 3
ORDER BY season_id, rk
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Join the two windowed subqueries so the N‑th batsman lines up with the N‑th bowler (same rk)
WITH top_bats AS (
      SELECT m.season_id,
             b.player_id,
             SUM(b.runs_scored) AS total_runs,
             ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY m.season_id
                                ORDER BY SUM(b.runs_scored) DESC, b.player_id) AS rk
      FROM   batsman_scored AS b
      JOIN   match          AS m ON m.match_id = b.match_id
      GROUP  BY m.season_id, b.player_id
),
top_bowl AS (
      SELECT m.season_id,
             w.player_out   AS player_id,
             COUNT(*)       AS total_wkts,
             ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY m.season_id
                                ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, w.player_out) AS rk
      FROM   wicket_taken AS w
      JOIN   match        AS m ON m.match_id = w.match_id
      WHERE  w.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'hit wicket', 'retired hurt')
      GROUP  BY m.season_id, w.player_out
)
SELECT tb.season_id,
       tb.player_id      AS batsman_id,
       tb.total_runs,
       tw.player_id      AS bowler_id,
       tw.total_wkts
FROM   top_bats AS tb
JOIN   top_bowl AS tw
  ON   tb.season_id = tw.season_id
 AND   tb.rk        = tw.rk
ORDER  BY tb.season_id, tb.rk
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Attach names to player IDs for a clearer result (still keeping limit 20)
